-- A further variant of the same driving example as
-- `generic_call_composes_via_body_substitution.tr`: `trunc(b, 4)` (an
-- EXPLICIT width) replaced with `trunc(b)` (1-arg, implicit width).
--
-- `trunc`'s 1-arg form has no width of its own -- `type_call`
-- (`types/expr.rs`) now threads the write's own declared LHS type
-- (`b : [5]`) into the call as a `hint`, letting `Double`'s generic
-- `n` solve from matching its return shape (`[n + 1]`) against that
-- hint: `n = 4`. This is PURELY type-level (no value-range reasoning
-- at all) -- `bounds.rs`'s own `trunc` arm still separately re-proves
-- that the resolved width is actually lossless under `b`'s own
-- guard-narrowed range (`[2, 16)` under `b < 0b1111`), exactly as it
-- does for the explicit-width form.
--
-- That second, independent check matters: a width solved PURELY from
-- matching return-type shapes has no inherent connection to whether
-- the value being truncated actually fits. `bounds.rs` rejects (rather
-- than silently masks) an implicit `trunc` whose inferred width can't
-- be proven lossless -- caught during this feature's own review via a
-- `Double(x:[n]):[n+2]` case assigned into an UNGUARDED `[5]`-wide
-- target, which would otherwise silently discard real bits with no
-- diagnostic at all.
module M {
    in a : [1]
    out b : [5] where _ > 1 = 5

    Double(x : [n]) : [n + 1] { return x << 1 }

    rule step {
        a?
        b < 0b1111
        b := Double(trunc(b))
    }
}
